  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Discreet and barely visible from the road, Pramousquier beach spreads out as an expanse of fine white sand and peacock-blue water, in the sheltered bay between Cap Nègre (which protects it from the Mistral) and Pointe de l'Écuelle. On the night of 14 to 15 August 1944, the Commandos d'Afrique climbed the rocks of Cap Nègre at Pramousquier, where a plaque recalls their landing (Le Lavandou tourist office).</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The beach is shared between two towns, which do not have the same rule. On the Rayol-Canadel-sur-Mer side, dogs on a leash are allowed from 1 June to 30 September between 7pm and 8am only (town hall, 23 June 2026). On the Le Lavandou side, which ranks Pramousquier among its "12 beaches", the town hall's official page says beach access is "strictly forbidden to dogs and all animals from 1 April to 30 October", while the Le Lavandou tourist office goes the same way: its 2026 booklet of the 12 beaches gives dogs allowed from 1 November to 31 March, and the bathing profile of the natural beach of Pramousquier reminds that pets are banned in the swimming area. Only 31 October is not covered in the same way by the two texts. To settle in with your dog in summer, therefore stay on the Rayol-Canadel side and respect the 7pm-8am hours; check the signs on site to spot the boundary between the two towns. Rayol-Canadel specifies that guide and assistance dogs, identified and on a leash, remain welcome at all times, and that owners must pick up droppings.</p>
      <p>On the practical side, the Gulf of Saint-Tropez tourist office describes a beach of fine white sand in a bay sheltered from the mistral by Cap Nègre. By car, take the D559 towards Le Lavandou, turn right just after the Beau Site hotel, park in the service lane, cross the road and go down several narrow flights of steps to the sand: so plan for a dog that copes with steps, or use the Voie Verte. The Le Lavandou tourist office adds that the beach is 400 metres long, nicknamed "la Mystérieuse" because it is reached by small footpaths, and that parking is very limited; a bus stop is under 500 metres away. The town bans smoking on its beaches and removes bins from the sand: bring your own waste bags, as biodegradable bag dispensers are due to be installed in the town.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Voie Verte (former railway line) from Canadel is a car-free route on foot or by bike (about 2 km according to the tourist office); the town reminds visitors that pedestrians have priority on the Voie Verte, so keep your dog close.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p><a href="/en/fiche-canadel">Canadel beach</a> is 2 km away along the Voie Verte.</p>
